e RAN4 definition tables essentially unchanged, grouping exceptions together by type (harmonic interference, cross-band isolation, etc.). For RAN5, test requirement tables should clearly define the required RF settings (channel, bandwidth, RBs, DL power, etc.) such that no additional tables need referencing. Test requirement tables should also contain an embedded pointer (presumably copied from the RAN4 document) that points directly to the exception definition tables in RAN4, for traceability. Figure 3 Challenges of manual cross-checking between TS 38.101-1 cl 7.3A.4 and TS 38.521-1 Cl 7.3A.0.4 for every exception Observation 4: Reference Sensitivity-exception applicability today requires cross-referencing independently-numbered RAN4 core tables and RAN5 conformance tables carrying duplicated content on a per-band/per-combination basis, which does not scale well for 6G multi-CC cases. Proposal 4: RAN4 keeps its own exception definition tables grouped by exception type, largely unchanged. RAN5 test-requirement tables fully specify the required RF test settings inline and contain an embedded pointer back to the corresponding RAN4 exception definition table, to keep core specification to test specification traceability without requiring cross-checking multiple, independently numbered tables.

